Subject: [PATCH 0039] tx: allow estimated-RD transform-type pruning below speed 4

THE FINDING

get_tx_mask() chooses between two transform-type pruning regimes:

    if (num_allowed > 2 && sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_tx_type_est_rd) {
        prune_txk_type() / prune_txk_type_separ()     <- intra AND inter
    } else {
        if (prune_2d_txfm_mode >= TX_TYPE_PRUNE_1 && is_inter && ...)
            prune_tx_2D()                             <- inter ONLY
    }

prune_tx_type_est_rd is 0 at speeds 0-3. At those presets INTRA blocks take
the else branch, fail its is_inter test, and receive NO cost-based
transform-type pruning at all. They get set reduction
(use_reduced_intra_txset) but nothing that ranks candidates by estimated
cost.

Every candidate that survives then pays a full 8-state TCQ trellis in
av2_optimize_b, and under TCQ that trellis cannot be skipped -- the
dequantizer is a state machine driven by coefficient parity, so
coefficients that did not come out of the trellis dequantize incorrectly.
A callgrind profile of the current tip puts the TCQ trellis at roughly 40%
of retired instructions at cpu-used=2 (29.96% in
av2_trellis_loop_diagonal_st8_avx2 alone), which makes the number of
surviving transform-type candidates the dominant cost driver at that
preset.

The mechanism proposed here is not new or speculative: it is already in
production at speeds 4 and 5.

MEASURED, NOT READ

The resolved values at the point of use, dumped from an instrumented build
(320x192, --limit=2, qp=110):

    speed  prune_tx_type_est_rd  prune_2d_txfm_mode  winner_mode_tx_type_pruning
      0            0                    1                      0
      1            0                    1                      0
      2            0                    2                      0
      3            0                    2                      0
      4            1                    4                      1
      5            1                    4                      1
      6            0                    5                      2

Three separate transform-type pruning mechanisms all switch on together in
the speed 3 -> 4 step.

WHY THE OVERRIDE IS PLACED LAST

prune_tx_type_est_rd is assigned in two other places in this same function
(= 1 under speed >= 4, = 0 under speed >= 6). The encoder has a documented
history of speed-feature edits that a later writer silently overwrites --
upstream db16baa fixed exactly that for uneven 4-way partitions, where an
earlier attempt "was not working as intended" because the field was set
elsewhere. Assigning at the very end of the setter makes this override the
last writer by construction rather than by reading the file and hoping.

Build with -DAVM_DUMP_TX_SF=1 and the encoder prints the RESOLVED value, so
the arm can be checked rather than assumed. Note the first call to this
setter does not carry the configured preset -- it reports speed=0 whatever
--cpu-used says -- so the dump prints on change and reports both
cpi->oxcf.speed and the setter's parameter. A print-once diagnostic latches
onto that first call and reports the wrong thing.

ARMS

    (default, no flags)                  identical to today's encoder;
                                         the override block compiles out
    -DAVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED=3          adds the estimated-RD regime at speed 3
    -DAVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED=2          ... and at speed 2   <- the arm of interest

Speed 6 turns the flag back off upstream and the override preserves that.
That is consistent rather than contradictory: by speed 6 the candidate set
is usually small enough that the num_allowed > 2 test fails, so the
estimate stops repaying its own cost.

THIS IS AN APPROXIMATION, NOT A FREE WIN

prune_txk_type() calls av2_cost_coeffs_txb_laplacian, which is itself 1.09%
of retired instructions at speed 5 in the profile. The patch buys fewer
trellis calls by paying for a cheaper estimate, so it has a real cost as
well as a real saving and it will move BD-rate. It is a ratio question --
speedup% / BD-rate% against the speed-2 bar of 30 -- which only CTC can
answer.

It is also orthogonal to the pre-trellis RD gate (patches 0027 / 0035):
that gate prunes AFTER quantization, this prunes BEFORE the forward
transform. They can be run together or separately.

VERIFICATION

The default build must be byte-identical to the unpatched encoder, since
the override compiles out entirely. That is checked by .obu md5 across
preset x qindex and recorded in experiments/registry.csv.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/speed_features.c b/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
index 546b318..0361631 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
@@ -702,6 +702,68 @@ static void set_good_speed_features_framesize_independent(
     sf->inter_sf.enable_six_param_warp_in_winner_mode_by_tid =
         cm->current_frame.pyramid_level >= 3 ? 1 : 0;
   }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // Experiment: extend estimated-RD transform-type pruning below speed 4.
+  //
+  // get_tx_mask() picks between two pruning regimes:
+  //
+  //   if (num_allowed > 2 && prune_tx_type_est_rd)
+  //       prune_txk_type() / prune_txk_type_separ()   <- intra AND inter
+  //   else if (prune_2d_txfm_mode >= TX_TYPE_PRUNE_1 && is_inter && ...)
+  //       prune_tx_2D()                                <- inter ONLY
+  //
+  // prune_tx_type_est_rd is 0 at speeds 0-3, so at those presets INTRA blocks
+  // reach the else branch, fail its is_inter test, and get no cost-based
+  // transform-type pruning at all. Every surviving candidate then pays a full
+  // 8-state TCQ trellis, and the trellis is ~40% of retired instructions at
+  // speed 2.
+  //
+  // This block is placed at the very end of the setter deliberately. The flag
+  // is written in two other places in this function (= 1 under speed >= 4,
+  // = 0 under speed >= 6), and the encoder has a history of speed-feature
+  // edits that a later writer silently overwrites -- see upstream db16baa for
+  // the same bug class. Assigning last makes the override the last writer by
+  // construction rather than by reading the file.
+  //
+  // Set AVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED to the lowest preset that should use the
+  // estimated-RD regime. 4 reproduces today's encoder exactly.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED
+#define AVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED 4
+#endif
+#if AVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED < 4
+  // Speed 6 turns the flag back off upstream; preserve that.
+  if (speed >= AVM_TX_EST_RD_MIN_SPEED && speed < 6)
+    sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_tx_type_est_rd = 1;
+#endif
+
+  // Self-check for the multi-writer trap: build with -DAVM_DUMP_TX_SF=1 and the
+  // encoder prints the RESOLVED value once per run. Confirm it reads 1 at the
+  // preset under test before trusting any timing number from this arm.
+#if defined(AVM_DUMP_TX_SF) && AVM_DUMP_TX_SF
+  {
+    // This setter is called more than once per encode, and the FIRST call does
+    // not carry the configured preset -- it reports speed=0 whatever
+    // --cpu-used says. A print-once diagnostic latches onto that call and
+    // reports the wrong thing, which is the same "you measured the value you
+    // did not mean" failure this dump exists to prevent. So print on change,
+    // and print the configured preset alongside the parameter.
+    static int last_speed = -1, last_flag = -1;
+    if (speed != last_speed ||
+        sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_tx_type_est_rd != last_flag) {
+      last_speed = speed;
+      last_flag = sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_tx_type_est_rd;
+      fprintf(stderr,
+              "[AVM_TX_SF] oxcf.speed=%d setter_speed=%d "
+              "prune_tx_type_est_rd=%d prune_2d_txfm_mode=%d "
+              "winner_mode_tx_type_pruning=%d\n",
+              cpi->oxcf.speed, speed,
+              sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_tx_type_est_rd,
+              (int)sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.prune_2d_txfm_mode,
+              sf->tx_sf.tx_type_search.winner_mode_tx_type_pruning);
+    }
+  }
+#endif
 }
 
 // Define frame size independent speed features for low complexity decoding
